<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Okay so I'm gonna be upfront: a lot of my comments here are going to be representative of this being an unfinished work.</p>
<p>The core problem I have with this at the moment is nothing interesting happens: all the interesting events are elided so completely that they have no impact. For example, consider:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hey I’ve worked with SCP-████ before back when I was a researcher, that’s a fucking safe class SCP how in fucks name did it breach containment?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This implies interesting things! There's a safe class anomaly that somehow breached containment but&#8230;nothing really happens here? There's no hint at what the anomaly is so we can't theorise how it might've breached containment, but there's also no hint at what it breaching containment means like, it could be a harmless AI that rolls around in a roomba, or it could be a button that kills everyone if pressed.</p>
<p>The addendums after the exploration log suffer from this a lot: having Cutting request extra help is you saying 'hey this thing is dangerous' when really you should be showing us it being dangerous.</p>
<p>My biggest concern though is that I've read all of what you have so far and I don't know where the story is going: at the moment, it just feels really aimless, and it seems like this is in part because you don't have an aim here. There's a lot of elements that could go somewhere interesting though and it just feels like it needs to decide whether the focus is 'ancient bronze age civilisation', 'powerful being who can destroy whatever' or 'ancient foundation site moved from the present'. I think either the first or the last could work, but it needs to explore it more, I think? My concern with doing the second is you start getting into big <a href="/scp-1730">SCP-1730</a> territory and frankly you want to steer this as far away from that as you can.</p>
<p>On the last: a lot of this treats the site as if it was just&#8230;moved recently but there's also a lot of places that treat is as mysterious and unknown and it feels like this really needs to settle on handling it in one of the two ways.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>No barriers are to be placed around SCP-XXXX due to risk of discovery by satellites or civilians.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems a little counter-intuitive; like, surely you could add barriers a few km away say and then just be all 'radiation leak' or 'dangerous naturally occurring gas' or w/e and it would be more secure?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The area and surroundings that SCP-XXXX occupies</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'area' and 'surroundings' seem basically synonymous here and I'd just use one</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An underground facility designated Site-██-█ has been constructed near SCP-XXXX for immediate analysis of artefacts or relevant information retrieved from SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is the site redacted? Surely anyone cleared to know about this is also cleared to know where the site is and indeed likely works there?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any incursion that on-site security is incapable of responding to is to be immediately reported to Site Director Gerald or if necessary, to Site-██.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This phrasing implies that incursions site security can respond to are not to be immediately reported which is a bad idea; but also this feels like something a reader can assume rather than something necessary to say.</p>
<p>Also, if you redact the site people are to report stuff to, how are people meant to know who to report stuff to?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Aerial view of the ████████ of SCP-XXXX recovered from downed Chaos Insurgency drone, it is unknown how said drone was not affected by SCP-XXXX’s effect</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why is someone allowed to see this picture but not see what it is? Why is a reader being shown this picture if we aren't meant to know what it is?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>resembling a technologically advanced foundation facility.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'foundation' should be capitalised here</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Footnote 1: The object is highly unpredictable and possesses considerable destructive capabilities. A containment breach may escalate into a K-Class scenario, and the object's recontainment is to be considered a high priority. Often assigned to Euclid and Keter class objects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is badly placed; I'd probably put it after the end of 'containment breach' but I'd also refocus it on describing what that means in terms of a containment breach, since opening on 'The object is highly unpredictable' is extremely confusing to a reader because we're not sure what that has to do with containment breaches.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 is a currently undiscovered SCP</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The previous sentence basically states this already.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>that possesses the ability to manipulate space and time and appears to have caused the containment breach that led to the creation of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd add a semi-colon/comma after 'time' just to separate 'space and time' and 'appears' more</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The O5 council has ordered the immediate termination of SCP-XXXX-1 as soon as it is discovered (see Protocol-XXXX).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels weird; it feels like it needs a little more setup to establish why but??</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-2 was an unknown Bronze Age civilisation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This needs something more hinting at why it's related to the core scp before introducing it here: I'd probably lead with like, SCP-XXXX-1 wiped out the civilisation say.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Footnote 2: Possibly an offshoot of the ██████ civilisation but is still under speculation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>this is a shit redaction. Anything you put in the blackbox is gonna be far more interesting than an open void.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>but was wiped out in a mass genocide by SCP-XXXX-1.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>genocide implies mass.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1's motivation to do so is unclear. (see exploration logs).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is&#8230;.just a weird sentence to include? It feels like this should be where you hook a reader into finding out more about what's happening but it's just so blandly stated that I don't find myself caring about the mystery. Also the first period is unnecessary and I really don't like 'see exploration logs' style bits in a SCP.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is in relatively good condition despite both its age</p>
</blockquote>
<p>you have to indicate that SCP-XXXX is old before you say 'despite its age' otherwise a reader is like 'huh what?': it feels like the opening paragraph should set this up rather than like, getting distracted for two paragraphs explaining stuff before bringing it up.</p>
<p>Also what does 'relatively good' mean here? It's needlessly vague phrasing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is surrounded by the ruins of structures created by SCP-XXXX-2. (See analysis of surrounding structures)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>janky full stop before parenthesis again</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is unknown how or why SCP-XXXX came to be in the ████ desert as Site-██ is currently located at [REDACTED], it is hypothesised that SCP-XXXX-1 used the rotation of the earth to transport Site-██ due to [REDACTED]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Too much redaction.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No personnel were recovered alive from SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a weird statement because it's pretty unclear as to whether it means 'no-one has ever returned from SCP-XXXX alive' or 'we didn't find anyone alive inside SCP-XXXX' and it's also unclear why we'd expect to find someone alive inside SCP-XXXX like&#8230;it's 5000 years old?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One foundation guard by the name of Nathaniel ███████</p>
</blockquote>
<p>One weird trick to make a character a lot more relatable easily: don't redact their surname.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Footnote 4: As of writing this article there are no plans on changing the D-class identification system</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't quite land for me because there's not been anything establishing what the D-Class identification system in use is so it being different doesn't necessarily land</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Discovery</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't mean to bang this drum again, but every single redaction in this section is a bad redaction with the exception of only &quot;SCP-████&quot;. This includes the two [DATA EXPUNGED]'s too.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>3 days after the acquisition of SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'acquisition' feels like a weird way to say this since it's a facility and you can't quite, acquire it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>loud and clear, Confirm all members</p>
</blockquote>
<p>random capital</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and can't really confirm exactly how far below the Site stretches, it's very possible this is another reality shifting anomaly</p>
</blockquote>
<p>the 'really' feels clunky here, as does leaving 'ground' implied rather than explicit. alternatively 'down' would work better than 'below'.</p>
<p>Also it feels like this comma should be a . or at least a ;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>evac outta there understood?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>comma before understood</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
</div>
</div>

